    "lawCode": "Amd §13, Work Comp L",
    "lawSection": "Workers' Compensation Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S4631A\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\nAn act to amend the workers' compensation law, in relation to the estab-\nlishment of rates of payment and delivery of health care services\n\nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\nTo require the Workers' Compensation Board to biennially prepare and\nestablish rate schedules for health care rendered under the workers'\ncompensation system and to require the board to publish, within 30 days\nof enactment of this legislation, the existing schedule of rates.\n\nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\nSection 1 of the bill requires the Workers' Compensation Board to bien-\nnially prepare and establish rate schedules for health care delivered\nunder the workers' compensation system. Section 2 requires the board to\npublish the existing schedule within 30 days of the legislation taking\neffect and subsequent biennial schedules by January 15. Section 3 is the\neffective date.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:\nThe Workers' Compensation Board has not adjusted rates of payment for\nhealth care providers for over ten years. Today, New York's rates are\nthe lowest or near the lowest in the country for many health care\nservices. Payments for health care services rendered pursuant to the\nworkers' compensation system do not reflect the current cost for provid-\ning health care, including increased labor, property and energy costs.\nAs a result, numerous providers no longer participate in the workers'\ncompensation system, and those that do struggle to maintain quality\nservices in light of the low payment levels.  The bill would require the\nWorkers' Compensation Board to undertake the process of examining the\nappropriateness of rates of payment biennially, something the Board is\ncurrently not required to do.\n\nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n2007-08 A.7779-A/S.5209 Referred to Labor\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\nNone.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nImmediately.\n\n",
